026-09-11T14:58:03Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:58:03+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:06+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Some conjectures of Graffiti.pc on total domination\" \"proof\" \"radius\" OR \"rad\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe paper titled **\"Some conjectures of Graffiti.pc on total domination\"** was authored by Ermelinda DeLaViña, Qi Liu, Ryan Pepper, Bill Waller, and Douglas B. West[1][2]. It was published in the *Congressus Numerantium* (Volume 185, 2007, pp. 81–95)[3][4].\n\n### Key Details\n*   **Topic:** The paper explores various conjectures generated by the computer program **Graffiti.pc** concerning the **total domination number** ($\\gamma_t(G)$) of a graph, often relating it to other graph invariants such as the **radius** ($rad(G)$ or $r(G)$), girth, and degree sequences[3][4].\n*   **Conjectures Involving Radius:** The paper discusses several bounds involving the radius of a graph. For instance, it references conjectures like:\n    *   If $G$ is connected, then $\\gamma_t(G) \\geq 2r(G)/\\delta(G)$[3][5].\n    *   It also examines other bounds where the domination number is related to the radius, such as the observation that the domination number is at least two-thirds of the radius[6][4].\n*   **Research Context:** This work is part of a broader effort to use automated conjecture-generating programs (like Graffiti and its successors) to identify significant, provable inequalities in graph theory[7][8]. The paper provides analysis and potential proofs or counterexamples for these machine-generated statements[4][9].\n\nIf you are looking for specific proofs for these conjectures, note that while the paper discusses these relationships, many Graffiti conjectures have been the subject of ongoing research and formal verification in the years since the paper's publication[10][11].\n\nSources:\n[1] [google.lu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFdwCkMHQGJL5ByuLhh2HH9Z_Nnic05GVsU4vY1Q3YJlsovhi0Qs