{"step_index":76,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:59:06Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:59:06+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:59:09+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Henning\" \"Yeo\" \"Total Domination in Graphs\" \"rad(G)\" OR \"radius\" \"proof\"\" returned the following summary:\nIn the monograph *Total Domination in Graphs* (2013) by Michael A. Henning and Anders Yeo, the authors provide several fundamental bounds relating the total domination number, $\\gamma_t(G)$, to other graph invariants, including the radius of a graph, $\\text{rad}(G)$[1][2].\n\nSpecifically, they state the following result:\n\n**Proposition:** If $G$ is a connected graph of order at least two, then $\\gamma_t(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G)$[1].\n\n### Context and Related Bounds\nThe book serves as a primary reference for total domination theory[3][2]. In addition to the inequality involving the radius, the text includes several related lower bounds for the total domination number:\n\n*   **Diameter:** For a connected graph $G$ of order at least two, $\\gamma_t(G) \\ge \\frac{\\text{diam}(G) + 1}{2}$[1].\n*   **Girth:** For a graph with girth $g$, $\\gamma_t(G) \\ge g/2$[1].\n*   **Domination Number:** For a graph with no isolated vertices, $\\gamma(G) \\le \\gamma_t(G) \\le 2\\gamma(G)$[1][4].\n\nThe proofs for these and other results are contained within the technical sections of the monograph, which covers both algorithmic aspects and structural graph theory related to total domination[5][2]. For the formal proof of the radius bound, readers are typically referred to the specific chapter addressing lower bounds in the *Total Domination in Graphs* text[1][2].\n\nSources:\n[1] [jomardpublishing.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG8l9G4ovd4jM9AwTGChCPa6yk6C6JvfTaA1t_ZWkijOPINBjjWunShT66sttTJG7ajCMyJf_dkJrXyqVCHdwRtrGnhKJRmUjgBBUeKzMQaztYbN9e-Lh_9qm8Xfdk-xF9LItqZ2A0-_maIBpJO-EuKG2vH8th-QOQkaFyA8tD56-hUt_eckQ==)\n[2] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHpBpzaajf9zR2NCZed89Zrp6PxkipLBHMOWXUsUOrwu9VNnPp89JOVg43ofBKRC-7ijM89WyZs9KKsUCdUS6ruWvE1jJWP9zMETlb5-Pov7DrqAF8WuEiCBaxdK8dVou9Nx2pQxGBUjqv9XROC_w99S1IYrtx6kter)\n[3] [dokumen.pub](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGEOdYsWTLZyW6DKcXN2etGkDGoK5DHZ_FCGygrIL6aqFfhcZ2HVVhxRUD--1MLPBaJEjbJltjNHQPJCgwlSwsTucCDZOucpZ8h2Wvc_8D5kp9hkmhwwGyfIOcS0MjjHpdLEDYVhpE9YUsOCcsA3QlxcLaMql5YVAgxYDEL2cGf1zAcB3T1q7y6n0wpS5PZVHm0yESGYOixRju9ePCS0o1FwdzjQ2l0T0MzeCClxjrN)\n[4] [wmich.edu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG-9EPsw_TRS-mN7vbZy9hxWp5XtsWSl4RNc-2MblVf9cvJKGKTr9jYWe394TN9f4DkRpV593H7M9_DAsmQvrPnJT6Ny6mAO1Sbg43bigRwi-Cnj28K69UphbInZwgQwVhAv9VSv9HH___klw5QswImHO7bus6Aoj-6nmeWaoXnxpYPS59hTVKPtas=)\n[5] [the-ica.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHP8VejeNbD_rP0t2Pj37UMbiXVkHA92fPm4MUehjzN6Ez20DPfhFl8N8RQquAx9meoOy-gJwP-Wl17P25beopGqyCoW95u1WZGeWbWMiyBlJ9NlDIgPsczAatVmMQ6cAY7yAPO5EuCV0efi7OjSwe8g57ervBMbMIiJg==)"}
{"step_index":77,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:59:09Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"\\\"Total Domination in Graphs\\\" \\\"rad(G)\\\" \\\"Proposition\\\" OR \\\"Theorem\\\" OR \\\"Proof\\\"\"","toolAction":"\"Searching for proof text\"","toolSummary":"\"Search proof text in Henning Yeo\""}}]}
{"step_index":78,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:59:12Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:59:12+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:59:16+02:00\nThe search for \"\"Total Domination in Graphs\" \"rad(G)\" \"Proposition\" OR \"Theorem\" OR \"Proof\"\" returned the following summary:\nIn graph theory, the relationship between the **total domination number** $\\gamma_t(G)$ and the **radius** $\\text{rad}(G)$ of a connected graph $G$ is a well-studied topic. Several results establish lower bounds for the total domination number in terms of the radius and diameter of the graph.\n\n### Key Results and Theorems\n\n*   **General Lower Bound:** It is a known result in the study of total domination that for any connected graph $G$ of order $n \\ge 2$ (with no isolated vertices), the total domination number is bounded below by the radius[1][2]:\n    $$\\gamma_t(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G)$$\n*   **Total Domination Integrity:** Research into \"Total Domination Integrity\" (TDI)—a parameter related to the total domination number—also utilizes the radius in its bounds[3][4]. Specifically:\n    *   **Theorem:** For a connected graph $G$ of order $n > 2$ with no isolated vertices, $\\text{TDI}(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G) + 1$[3][4].\n*   **Distance $k$-Domination:** The bound $\\gamma_t(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G)$ has been generalized to the total distance $k$-domination number, $\\gamma_{t,k}(G)$[1]. For $k \\ge 1$:\n    $$\\gamma_{t,k}(G) \\ge \\frac{1}{k} \\text{rad}(G)$$\n    This is derived by noting that a spanning tree $T$ exists such that $\\gamma_{t,k}(G) = \\gamma_{t,k}(T)$ and that $\\text{rad}(G) \\le \\text{rad}(T)$[1].\n\n### Contextual Notes\n*   **Total Domination Number ($\\gamma_t(G)$):** Defined as the minimum cardinality of a set $S \\subseteq V(G)$ such that every vertex in $V(G)$ is adjacent to at least one vertex in $S$ (requiring no isolated vertices)[5][6][7].\n*   **Radius ($\\text{rad}(G)$):** Defined as the minimum eccentricity among all vertices in $G$, where the eccentricity of a vertex is the maximum distance to any other vertex in the graph[8][9][10].\n*   **Applications:** These bounds are frequently used in structural graph th\n<truncated 1034 bytes>\nb-u7onMIut7L_Oz9IEw3LqBpFQSZQfsmZr9X9IbAqzDuA4ywjdgSIfcT8nyhek63awI2exSc_CTwrIl4i8le5rJqPqTEAr7j-GyQbt8PpfvA9stGlKglTNvQt41s3NjLk9R73yd9rNDg=)\n[5] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG3xwc3X-6cOJEoeKR8ldbxONu8pfmnAtt4uJkkgc1J2pB6O-yBrXXa07XWZAyLDi-wHDauNhLA1Zfsm4vPqMR06Uo0ORCP2H8SJ1LmR7SzC7T2NiOQ14_jXNCvJ-ouqdigtBSPLm5V3KVp5B3CAByDtDBhI35gpnwxiVLibOtVjbb2Sw84vC9xjQ==)\n[6] [uni-ulm.de](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG5bxPlBU43jIpX0yPa87dBiW6-HESAj4gF93k16yLNShfkSR5JmvTJF9T2C3zKFA3vPv0MS1lR62IJ0OuOokeXIeTKH8yY-SVqsTOXmeO7UQ-Eu9E2GZQ7rrHYrzenjGhucuWTK6xLEDSH-y-l8RP1ojcMMRh1VAUp_Mdu_cgFz39vbbfe9sKipsfyvzOXimYjDY3nwYU8EHZsLhMXVEGE-ZQ8Uw_4yrTLqsI19ImGRfXHur_rZhffrXJHJs8=)\n[7] [uz.zgora.pl](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGmLb5-ktXhNqsYWtvfL-okvb-ZiH5tEaDmI4YrOcZy9TjoVrwYpqddshV4XCzvIE6FUYaNEt_aylxAh2ZVT9S8qz4DE-T2LTWdM4-UbugceZWGQtxnjNbeOdufkdhJfEjUou9MmynIfFSIjw==)\n[8] [e-bookshelf.de](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHLpQjoR0GP2uh483VE0D13omonZnDQ5-yLr_3g0zu8vq3vu6x6be0vnYgVhaF0350_1bS_MqVV9SCPN3u_6vFaZVxEypwYbDEm801V3kbCQqW_a68Xx11hHGAkNFYri3y8UMlTUHulYzcbesXJ-yfvBd3hNaWq8OBSrb-g)\n[9] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGeKkWN20cJ2Gn-9oPrOa2Hqnzxz1wNsIiieFE-G3P0_7Kcr4YJrVCOIQCzj4d4pCkwFGhj19y2hUpw4C6QoKNrASBo1JJt5NbDDvZiIsjm05Yw3yYcoBtDZskp6ppj0PpfIBzdlzzEKLG8FrihBIgoP_QU0UNKpPhsTvTM-btFy8RR-4HmoK0=)\n[10] [amazonaws.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEvlJl4hMpI3Sfgbky81PKiOVVu3eTzpQ9OMSmsgA3a7lht3Qk6ZSog4sUOrx4Z5zs1cNt9wwnLSZSo4ovoU9_2DlqfGQqyfThqFz5Uclpy4ZBpDp6HUBUxDAcVIIQM-c