{"step_index":80,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:59:18Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:59:18+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:59:21+02:00\nThe search for \"\"arxiv\" \"spanning tree\" \"rad(G) \\le rad(T)\" \"total\" \"domination\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe query appears to relate to research on graph theory, specifically involving **spanning trees**, **radius** (often denoted as $\\text{rad}(G)$), and **total domination** numbers.\n\nWhile a single paper containing the exact string `\"rad(G) <= rad(T)\"` alongside \"total domination\" was not directly identified in the search, several key results in this area of graph theory address the relationships between graph parameters and their spanning trees:\n\n*   **Total Domination and Spanning Trees:** It is a known result in graph theory that the total domination number of a connected graph is related to the total domination number of its spanning trees[1]. Research has explored various bounds and relationships between domination parameters of a graph $G$ and those of a spanning tree $T$ contained within $G$[2][1].\n*   **Radius and Spanning Trees:** The radius of a graph, $\\text{rad}(G)$, is the minimum eccentricity of any vertex in $G$. When considering a spanning tree $T$ of $G$, it is generally true that $\\text{rad}(G) \\le \\text{rad}(T)$, as any path in $G$ has a corresponding distance property, and $T$ (being a subgraph) can only increase or maintain the distances between vertices compared to $G$.\n*   **Contextual Literature:** Research on these topics is frequently published in journals such as *Discrete Mathematics*[3][4], *Discrete Applied Mathematics*[4], and on platforms like **arXiv**[3][5][2][1].\n\nIf you are looking for a specific theorem or paper, it may be helpful to check recent surveys on \"total domination in graphs\"[3][1] or literature specifically discussing \"spanning tree radius\" and \"total domination\" together, as these parameters are often studied in the context of bounding graph invariants.\n\nSources:\n[1] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHXeW7bJhR3VQmGJbhH-nbjsp-pJ9mw9W0kBKJ9h940NtoGXN6AB_gviLRmPEI_6w71XrCZ9HMlWxI6ijnUjBM_XYpToED29JeV0-A-rJkgOhOEiwwn3FWi)\n[2] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGxZfEcsGXZQxzLnGJQBP6GFHWoEc4kO1WjqF0ufxdngQ10ul3Yt4F4y21J6CHOu-jv8-6FiXQEl-8_AJhCZoVOVjydK27QpiluTHLWsbxOa6b7YOxIQiI=)\n[3] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQExqCbJiCokOBT1wd88MlOZBGvNnH3XhRBtl6OgMzyk_1FBfic0jOP9FeTvqqSkZBoreyz5Emz6lIQKVOXnJ5vqQ2qFJTgoTtxEXfFfu9T4wPDFg_gQ1wk8)\n[4] [ejgta.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEG35RW0oPhXuqhOg4raMmJmNT4tAOhq76rutYzZv_Ydq0o09ioUk_gq-hJNX2Y02Z1vKTv36YTsMMXq0PUc62Yj8ibmK9Tj9p8UXfh2L3dFdtGhcSOFdQ9NKi46_FTkub11drOMB7DGTWJjMc=)\n[5] [arxiv.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEMNRVahXyzpQkt2jB0Wl_gZLyMsRnCCErMt06R4Z6RYjMQ978G2WOLTyT01mqsc9bF5B2F8804JE3lD8BKy8o5Po9DhyIsHZ3LiTjjz00GaVA743G7B6y5)"}
{"step_index":81,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:59:21Z","thinking":"**Investigating Graph Radii Inequality**\n\nThe agent will examine source [1] to understand a graph theory concept. Specifically, the agent will explore why the radius of a graph is less than or equal to the total domination number of the graph. The agent sets up a proof: considering a connected graph with at least two vertices and a total dominating set S. The next step is to remember the definition of the dominating set.\n\n\n**Examining Vertex Eccentricity**\n\nThe focus shifts to finding a vertex within the total dominating set *S* with eccentricity at most the size of *S*, where the eccentricity of a vertex is the maximum distance from that vertex to any other vertex in the graph. The goal is to prove that the radius of the graph is less than or equal to the cardinality of the total dominating set. The agent will test the condition of choosing a vertex in *S* and its distance to another vertex *y* in *V*.\n\nThe condition that *S* is a total dominating set implies that every vertex *y* in *V* is adjacent to some vertex *s\\_y* in *S*, leading to a distance of at most 1. The approach involves exploring whether any vertex in *S* can have a maximum distance less than or equal to the size of *S* to all other vertices. If the induced subgraph *G[S]* is connected, then the diameter of *G[S]* is at most k-1 (where *|S| = k*).\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"\\\"gamma_t(G) \\\\ge \\\\text{rad}(G)\\\" OR \\\"gamma_t(G) \\\\ge rad(G)\\\" \\\"proof\\\" \\\"Let S be a\\\"\"","toolAction":"\"Searching web for proof text\"","toolSummary":"\"Search proof text of gamma_t >= rad\""}}]}
{"step_index":82,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:59:27Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:59:27+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:59:32+02:00\nThe search for \"\"gamma_t(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G)\" OR \"gamma_t(G) \\ge rad(G)\" \"proof\" \"Let S be a\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe inequality $\\gamma_t(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G)$ is a known lower bound for the total domination number of a connected graph $G$ with no isolated vertices[1][2][3]. \n\nWhile a single standard proof is often cited in graph theory literature (such as the work by DeLaViña et al. referenced in various surveys[1][2][3]), the phrasing \"Let $S$ be a...\" is the standard beginning for a proof by construction or contradiction involving a total dominating set.\n\n### Context of the Inequality\n*   **Definition:** For a connected graph $G$ with no isolated vertices, the total domination number $\\gamma_t(G)$ is the minimum cardinality of a set $S \\subseteq V(G)$ such that every vertex in $V(G)$ is adjacent to at least one vertex in $S$[4].\n*   **The Bound:** The inequality $\\gamma_t(G) \\ge \\text{rad}(G)$ relates the total domination number to the radius of the graph (the minimum eccentricity among all vertices)[5][2].\n*   **Generalizations:** This result has been extended in various ways, including generalizations to the **total distance $k$-domination number** ($\\gamma_{k}^t(G) \\ge \\text{rad}_k(G)$)[1][6][7].\n\n### Typical Proof Approach\nProofs of this nature typically proceed by:\n1.  **Selection:** Letting $S$ be a minimum total dominating set of $G$ (i.e., $|S| = \\gamma_t(G)$)[8][9][10].\n2.  **Exploitation of Structural Properties:** Using the fact that every vertex $v \\in V(G)$ must have a neighbor in $S$, and every vertex $s \\in S$ must have a neighbor in $S$[4][11].\n3.  **Distance Arguments:** Utilizing the distance between vertices in $S$ and the remaining vertices in $V(G)$ to show that the size of $S$ must be at least the radius of the graph[1][2]. \n\nIf you are looking for a specific proof text, it is likely found in foundational papers regarding total domination conjectures, such as those by **DeLaViña et al.** (often \n<truncated 1651 bytes>\nkfvoPjl8yVaUM9SSyaDBwvxiWBOMvnZQXahQ6OzMg4_MdEPh4qOnvbABOyobaSt9NpJ3eS7WOiDeGw039YygZTbM1GfUS_eKHVXQ_LrS9XvjTrQGCnPGoRDrWfjcQNeIgw05zT1lN421kBiWNw3zTBkbNbfz0k=)\n[7] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGeyoVvNh3PZvLZq1ipVMtLPYahLSmlhkR_xa1I3SGda3KyNlXfO6y8sfQbOleLtyhTf4_Bl7rEDoRkx-Bnta91jkdVITA6FZTaxCgNIzU1UCMnEvR3o-auP6UInSgfZABF7QYvzf2pHmrE9uZYvO-EAurbcPx6BIkpJ3HpYmNKEeL30rnVvDb902EsjNvq0TYasc7OWTd3CN47Ajo7KWKtMMM=)\n[8] [uni-lj.si](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG7OLU4CtzCygXZIjifJfZuSvIa3G5DiBVxDYEH8MnCTrAfz1LLxsRVY8upgAE5QReC8RDlo6XvajKA_nXyDyzBwAZnOhph1qRscOt4J9COgR7YqbrYI1ALnFTt7b10uDpLXBIADOsPYrHlUX71FM5s)\n[9] [sciepub.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGKw-F9n2DSOrQR__pnUah0cj7Gp5CiSH9LnHsONp02spsbABk_e4N-ZPIPIR1JGSMO1mdqWKk8Xi43C3UQNd09lqquwWDOWV2OMoAUjGJEBpgWMwxlmjHFLXIbBS-ycIYssGu-9Q==)\n[10] [bibliotekanauki.pl](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFJAHtYrO2PoxJ44nENkKX99jsuLcsQ3JTJ9ItbiXYC-4xb-paxy-tuI4QzBl1x49cuHxRS7XFtRLYx-kAf8H77915ZFhw8-rta0nDHMzLG2mDtYAiQq-GtguRPjUXcZkpH4r33IhY=)\n[11] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHRMAE1GIbPmmchRgEZsn79gJ-yoPGf0zWUhL2dx8zJIui9jIT6kf7X1Y8BCNbAsqgnfHHivSiZ7zVdvrlq46v8w9J6FS6tlv6v3rCtz3E0yCKfEowBhHvMyVQCnRZSmEwNCqixegwrk64Z96Z7RGP0w1mqwtsGoO8rrm1U11dW-dWQCkk90kOc5pDssZA9vxAoIDL1b9oAihfj2zujX6_KWRk=)\n[12] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGpijAjHrKPdWAqxaxkxnTclvZ-NE9YJjhUX7wapEB-0ek0G8haUGQGen79_Z4YCoct2X1JE6tQ0Lc95XD_UJ3bzbX_8XCy2eMjS-NJ4Ka9NqLKZZyyCL2w1rNgTnudrt_hzQvmUbDzqcbbSUjpG29Se5j-YPGRqLG4WrlzPJ9JHZ-HmKeuF6eOd