{"step_index":104,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:00:16Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:00:16+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:00:16+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py`\nTotal Lines: 510\nTotal Bytes: 23725\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: \"\"\"\n2: pipeline/known_relations.py — curated known relations for the novelty filter.\n3: \n4: The base table in :mod:`pipeline.novelty` speaks a small symbolic vocabulary\n5: (``alpha``, ``gamma``, ``omega``, …) and covers the classical chains\n6: (Whitney, König–Egerváry, Gallai, …). This module *extends* it with a large,\n7: domain-curated list of known graph-invariant relations over the **domination**\n8: and **zero-forcing** families (``total_domination_number``,\n9: ``zero_forcing_number``, ``annihilation_number``, ``slater``, …) that the base\n10: table does not name — the relations a graph theorist already considers folklore\n11: or published, so that a CEGIS survivor restating one of them is flagged\n12: *known* rather than *novel*.\n13: \n14: The relations are kept here as **human-readable strings** (exactly the form a\n15: domain expert writes them), and parsed at import time into the\n16: ``(lhs, {rhs: coeff}, offset, class, name)`` tuples that\n17: ``pipeline.novelty.KNOWN_THEOREMS`` consumes. Parsing is deliberately\n18: conservative: anything that does not parse cleanly to a linear relation over the\n19: known vocabulary (nonlinear ``ceil`` forms, multi-class hypotheses, unsupported\n20: \"not K_n\" / degree-bounded hypotheses, malformed fragments) is **skipped** and\n21: left *novel* — the safe direction, since wrongly hiding a real conjecture is the\n22: only 